WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.740
The "Barbie" movie sells itself as having&nbsp;
something for everybody. But when it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.420
to Ken, that may not be entirely true! From&nbsp;
silky smooth chests to the meaning behind a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.660
"beach off," there's a lot about Barbie's&nbsp;
sidekick that only adults will get.
 
 
 &nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.700
In director Greta Gerwig movie, it's quickly&nbsp;
established that Barbie has a personal theme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.960
song of sorts — the Indigo Girls classic&nbsp;
"Closer to Fine." Ken initially seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.860
content to sing that song along with her. But&nbsp;
when he ventures out into the real world and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:30.240
discovers a certain version of masculinity,&nbsp;
Ken no longer likes the song. At that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.240
Ken discovers his own favorite&nbsp;
song, and it soon takes over as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.740
the anthem for Barbieland's transition into&nbsp;
Kendom: "Push" by the band Matchbox Twenty.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.540
Aside from director Gerwig being a fan, "Push"&nbsp;
was likely chosen because it sounds exactly like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.260
the kind of song a dude-bro would be obsessed&nbsp;
with and want to sing to a woman he is trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.580
to woo. But those already familiar with the&nbsp;
song from its mid-'90s heyday immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.600
realized there was a lot more going on there.&nbsp;
With lyrics like "I wanna take you for granted,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.160
the song gives us insight into Ken's state of&nbsp;
mind. After all, he had previously felt taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.640
advantage of by Barbie, so it makes sense&nbsp;
that Ken would like a song like "Push."

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.920
"But it's Barbie and Ken.&nbsp;
There is no just Ken."
 
 
 &nbsp;

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.360
While there seems to be at least a little bit of&nbsp;
body diversity among the Barbies in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.080
the Kens are all pretty stereotypical in&nbsp;
that they're uniformly thin, chiseled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.700
and completely devoid of body hair. It's most&nbsp;
apparent with the two main Kens, played by Ryan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.440
Gosling and Simu Liu's, given how often their&nbsp;
bare chests are visible. But it can also be seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.700
or not seen, on the arms and legs of all the other&nbsp;
Kens. There's no visible hair on their bodies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.300
despite the fact that some of these actors&nbsp;
probably have some body hair in real life.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.380
Sure enough, Liu confirmed to Allure that he&nbsp;
had to have his body waxed two different times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.980
while filming "Barbie" to ensure that&nbsp;
smooth, hairless look. But that's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.000
all that went into making the actors look like&nbsp;
human-sized Ken dolls. Liu explained to Allure:

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.320
"We had to look glossy and plastic and there was&nbsp;
a lot of body paint applied every single day."

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.220
Younger viewers probably wouldn't have thought&nbsp;
twice about all that hairless skin. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.040
those who have a few more years&nbsp;
on them certainly found themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.160
wondering how all these adult men&nbsp;
could look so smooth and shiny. 
 
 &nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.720
Ken and Barbie are only in the real world&nbsp;
for about 10 seconds before Barbie starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:07.740
to experience what it's like to have men openly&nbsp;
gawk at her body and make gross comments about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.280
it. When the pair encounter a group of&nbsp;
construction workers, the workers start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.840
making a bunch of inappropriate sexual remarks,&nbsp;
all of which are directed at Barbie. It's then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.980
that Barbie explicitly tells them that both&nbsp;
she and Ken are lacking in human genitalia.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.060
Before that, however, Ken and Barbie's&nbsp;
inability to have sex is hinted at in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:27.660
hilariously awkward way that probably flew&nbsp;
over the heads of younger audience members.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:31.260
Barbie is trying to get Ken to go home&nbsp;
so she can party with the other Barbies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.740
and he asks if he can stay the night.&nbsp;
She is confused, and inquires as to why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.160
he would want to sleep over. Of course, he&nbsp;
doesn't have an answer, and ends up just as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.300
baffled about why he even asked the question in&nbsp;
the first place. Later, Weird Barbie remarks,

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.120
"I'd like to see what smooth blob&nbsp;
[Ken's] packing in those shorts."

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.660
Weird Barbie's comment is further evidence&nbsp;
that the dolls have a vague sense of sexual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.840
attraction to one another even though&nbsp;
they don't really know what to do with it.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:52.920
"I'm coming with you."

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.100
"Okay." 
 
 
Barbie and Ken learn most of the same truths about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.900
the real world, only they absorb the information&nbsp;
in almost exactly opposite ways. Barbie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.340
disappointed to see that she hadn't fixed feminism&nbsp;
and that the world is in fact still a patriarchy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:09.360
Meanwhile, Ken couldn't be more thrilled to learn&nbsp;
that there is a place where men rule instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.500
women. While exploring Los Angeles on his own,&nbsp;
Ken stumbles into a building where multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.280
screens are showing visual representations&nbsp;
of masculinity throughout the years. Ken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.900
becomes especially fixated on one recurring&nbsp;
figure in that montage — Sylvester Stallone.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:26.160
After that, Ken bases much of what it means&nbsp;
to be a man on both Stallone himself and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.180
characters he plays. "Rocky" clearly makes&nbsp;
a major impression on him, as Ken is seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.020
wearing boxing gloves at one point when he first&nbsp;
arrives back in Barbieland. Ken's obsession with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:37.920
horses as a sign of masculinity could also&nbsp;
be connected to Rocky Balboa's nickname,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.360
"The Italian Stallion." But most obvious is&nbsp;
the white fur coat that Ken wears for a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.080
stretch of the movie, which is modeled after&nbsp;
a similar coat that Stallone is seen wearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.220
in the montage. Stallone was known to don&nbsp;
extravagant fur coats in the '80s, helping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.720
to popularize them as a statement of not only&nbsp;
hyper-masculinity but also wealth and success.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.280
"Did you bring your rollerblades?"

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.380
"I literally go nowhere without them."
 
 
Throughout some of the film, Barbie is in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:05.820
real world, searching for the girl that her doll&nbsp;
avatar belongs to. During her journey, she and Ken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.200
end up at a high school. While Barbie confronts&nbsp;
Sasha in the school cafeteria, Ken is left on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.280
own to explore the high school. Before long, he&nbsp;
finds his way to the library. It's there that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.900
comes across several fake but hilariously-titled&nbsp;
books about the history of patriarchy and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.340
guy stuff. The books have names like "The&nbsp;
Origins of Patriarchy" and "Man Wars."

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.580
It's more than likely that those books&nbsp;
are meant to poke fun at Jordan Peterson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:31.200
and other figures like him. Peterson's most&nbsp;
famous book — "12 Rules of Life" — has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.160
seen as pro-patriarchy, and he's been known&nbsp;
to complain about how masculinity is under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:39.300
attack in modern society. Peterson has also&nbsp;
claimed that people are afraid to admit that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.960
men hold the power because, in his view,&nbsp;
men are simply better at being in charge.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.280
There's little doubt that Peterson would see&nbsp;
the changeover from Barbieland to Kendom as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.240
step in the right direction. It wouldn't be the&nbsp;
first time that a film takes Peterson's ideals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.260
and mines them for villainy. For one, Olivia&nbsp;
Wilde was very upfront about the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.160
Peterson inspired Chris Pine's wretchedly&nbsp;
evil character in "Don't Worry Darling."

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:03.960
The opening scenes of "Barbie" show how all&nbsp;
of the Barbies in Barbieland are BFFs. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:08.100
all love each other, respect each other, and&nbsp;
celebrate each other's success. While that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:11.940
mostly seems true for the Kens as well,&nbsp;
Beach Ken and Tourist Ken seem to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.120
pretty bitter rivalry. Much of it stems&nbsp;
from them competing for the attention of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.900
Stereotypical Barbie. But it also seems like&nbsp;
they simply don't like each other very much.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:22.800
How do you settle a dispute in Barbieland?&nbsp;
Well, it's later established that weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.160
don't exist there. Moreover, the Kens&nbsp;
don't exactly seem like the type to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.060
get into vicious physical altercations. So&nbsp;
it's suggested that they have a beach-off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:34.260
which sounds innocent enough — until phrases&nbsp;
like this start getting thrown around:

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.360
"I would beach you off right now, Ken."

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:37.740
"I'll beach off with you anyday, Ken."

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:41.100
Sounds a little like they are saying something&nbsp;
else, doesn't it? There are only a few gags in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:44.820
"Barbie" that are meant to elicit chuckles from&nbsp;
the adults while going over the heads of children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:49.620
and the whole concept of getting "beached off"&nbsp;
is perhaps the dirtiest of them all. That said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:53.280
having a snippet of it included in some of&nbsp;
the trailers was definitely an indication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.180
that a little bit of grown-up naughtiness was&nbsp;
going to be snuck into this PG-13 movie.
 
 &nbsp;

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:00.180
In order to take Barbieland back from the Kens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.420
the Barbies have to be reprogrammed&nbsp;
back to their original selves. This way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:06.450
they're no longer the meek, subservient&nbsp;
women that they've been turned into.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:06.480
"Okay, ladies! Let's do this."

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.920
In one of the movie's funniest sequences, some&nbsp;
of the reprogrammed Barbies pretend to need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:15.180
reassurance about their looks or have something&nbsp;
mansplained to them. The point is so that no Ken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:19.200
doll can resist giving his full attention to them.&nbsp;
There are some obvious ploys, like the Barbie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:23.580
that pretends she never saw "The Godfather" and&nbsp;
needs a Ken to explain the entire movie to her.

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.700
"Leave the gun. Take the cannolis."

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:30.960
But one of the more obscure ones has a Ken&nbsp;
explaining the role that Stephen Malkmus played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.100
in the evolution of rock 'n' roll in relation&nbsp;
to Lou Reed and the Velvet Underground. Most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:39.180
know Reed and his former band, but only those&nbsp;
with a love of '90s indie music will recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.800
Malkmus as the frontman for celebrated rock&nbsp;
group Pavement. Pavement's biggest mainstream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:48.420
success occurred in the early-to-mid-'90s — and&nbsp;
even then, they never exactly lit up the charts.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.960
Malkmus responded to the shout-out&nbsp;
in an Instagram story, initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:55.800
teasing that he was going to get his lawyers&nbsp;
involved but saying the band is "pro-Greta"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.860
Gerwig. So he seemed to appreciate the&nbsp;
mentioning of his name in the movie.

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:03.120
"Barbie" is absolutely jam-packed&nbsp;
with references to pop culture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:07.560
especially films. The films mentioned range from&nbsp;
timeless classics like "The Wizard of Oz" all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:11.700
way down to obscure films from the French New&nbsp;
Wave. But there is one scene in particular that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:16.440
is almost entirely based on another sequence from&nbsp;
a different movie. It's far beyond a mere Easter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:20.940
egg or background reference and instead is an&nbsp;
outright love letter to the film that inspired it.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:24.840
Though the big song and dance battle between&nbsp;
the Kens initially starts on the beach, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:29.760
are all soon magically transported to a dream-like&nbsp;
space of blues and pinks, with everyone dressed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:33.900
black. While some have suggested that the outfits&nbsp;
recall what Danny Zuko wears in much of "Grease,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:38.400
the whole scene is a much more obvious tribute to&nbsp;
the iconic dream ballet sequence from "Singin' in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:43.440
the Rain." Released in 1952, the legendary musical&nbsp;
predates both Ken and Barbie by a few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.060
and is certainly one of the oldest pieces of pop&nbsp;
culture history that "Barbie" pays homage to.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.720
There have been a lot of different&nbsp;
variations of Barbie and Ken dolls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:55.080
over the years. Longtime Barbie fans had&nbsp;
a blast trying to see how many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:58.620
dolls they recognized as characters in&nbsp;
the movie, especially those that only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:02.100
had a quick line or two, or only appeared&nbsp;
in the background and said nothing at all.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:02.940
"And Alan!"

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:06.900
With few exceptions, almost every Barbie&nbsp;
and Ken mentioned in the movie was a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:11.040
doll at some point. Remarkably, this also&nbsp;
includes the canceled doll Sugar's Daddy Ken.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:16.080
Also referenced and standing alongside Ken in the&nbsp;
movie is Earring Magic Ken, who has an even wilder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.940
history that older viewers surely remember.&nbsp;
Upon his release in 1993, many were quick to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:25.260
identify that Earring Magic Ken's clothing was&nbsp;
clearly inspired by the fashions popular in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:29.400
gay club scene at the time. As for the ring the&nbsp;
doll wore around its neck, let's just say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:34.380
in addition to "Gay Ken," Earring Magic Ken was&nbsp;
also sometimes referred to as "C--- Ring Ken."

